INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4243,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-efforts-faster.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-efforts-faster.html','Restoration efforts are working, but we need to move faster to meet targets, says U.N. report','restorationeffortsareworkingbutweneedtomovefastertomeettargetssaysunreport','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-30T02:40:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','A U.N. report finds that 252 million hectares (ha) (623 million acres) across 149 countries are being restored, creating more than 1 million green jobs—the largest documented global restoration effort to date.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4244,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-machine-safer-cities.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-machine-safer-cities.html','Using machine learning to build safer cities','usingmachinelearningtobuildsafercities','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T23:40:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','In eThekwini, researchers have harnessed machine learning and satellite data to spot the city''s most landslide-prone zones. A new study published in Scientific Reports has found that urban development activities, including road construction, property development and vegetation loss, are significantly increasing landslide susceptibility across parts of eThekwini.',2.0,'classified',NULL,40);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4245,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-faraway-tectonic-gulf-california-rifting.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-faraway-tectonic-gulf-california-rifting.html','Faraway tectonic forces may have accelerated the Gulf of California rifting','farawaytectonicforcesmayhaveacceleratedthegulfofcaliforniarifting','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T23:10:02Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','When the Baja California peninsula broke from mainland Mexico nearly 12 million years ago, it gave rise to the Gulf of California. New research suggests that this process of continental rifting was greatly accelerated due to tectonic forces on the opposite side of the Pacific Ocean.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4246,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-monterey-bay.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-monterey-bay.html','Researchers observe the arrival of El Niño in Monterey Bay','researchersobservethearrivalofelninoinmontereybay','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T22:30:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','For more than 30 years, MBARI technology has recorded real-time data about Monterey Bay, from oceanographic conditions at the surface to biological surveys in the depths of Monterey Canyon. These data are integral to understanding our changing ocean and underpin the institute''s efforts to monitor the impacts of the incoming El Niño, which has been predicted to be among the strongest since the beginning of modern oceanography.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4247,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-antarctic-polynyas-sea-ice.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-antarctic-polynyas-sea-ice.html','Waves alter Antarctic polynyas by breaking up surrounding sea ice','wavesalterantarcticpolynyasbybreakingupsurroundingseaice','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T22:20:02Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Polynyas are one of Antarctica''s great counterintuitive features: recurring areas of open water surrounded by vast expanses of sea ice. These unusual environments are hot spots for sea-ice production, ocean–atmosphere exchange and biological productivity. They are commonly maintained by powerful winds that push newly formed ice away from the coast, repeatedly exposing the ocean to the freezing Antarctic atmosphere.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4248,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nature-fundamental-rethink.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nature-fundamental-rethink.html','Nature finance needs a fundamental rethink, researchers argue','naturefinanceneedsafundamentalrethinkresearchersargue','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T21:40:04Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','The authors call on governments, investors and conservation organizers to reimagine nature finance based on a central tenet: raising funds should be a means to an end, not an end in itself.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4249,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-european-rivers-retain-plastic-sea.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-european-rivers-retain-plastic-sea.html','European rivers retain far more plastic than they carry to the sea, study finds','europeanriversretainfarmoreplasticthantheycarrytotheseastudyfinds','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T21:10:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Plastic that enters the environment does not necessarily make its way to the sea via rivers. In fact, only a tiny fraction eventually reaches river mouths. Most plastic remains on land or within river systems.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4250,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-microplastic-viral-disease-severity-rainbow.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-microplastic-viral-disease-severity-rainbow.html','Microplastic shape can influence viral disease severity in rainbow trout','microplasticshapecaninfluenceviraldiseaseseverityinrainbowtrout','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T20:20:12Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','The shape of microplastics can influence how deadly a viral infection can be for fish, according to new research from William & Mary''s Virginia Institute of Marine Science (VIMS). The findings suggest that some forms of plastic pollution pose greater biological risks than others, offering scientists a potential path toward reducing the environmental impacts of microplastics.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4251,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africa-debunking-myth-europeans-plotted.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africa-debunking-myth-europeans-plotted.html','Who mapped Africa? Debunking the myth that Europeans alone plotted the continent','whomappedafricadebunkingthemyththateuropeansaloneplottedthecontinent','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T19:50:06Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','After the United Nations adopted a resolution in September 2026 endorsing a map that more fairly represents the size of Earth''s landmasses, author and journalist Moky Makura of Africa No Filter—a main sponsor of the Correct the Map initiative—asked: "What else about Africa is the world underestimating that''s also hidden in plain sight?"',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4252,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-fresh-microplastic-laden-sierra-storms.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-fresh-microplastic-laden-sierra-storms.html','Fresh as the microplastic-laden Sierra snow: Storms can deliver pollution to even remote locations','freshasthemicroplasticladensierrasnowstormscandeliverpollutiontoevenremotelocations','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T19:20:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','For the first time, UC Riverside scientists have measured how much microplastic pollution falls on the Sierra Nevada during snowstorms and were surprised to find significant amounts even in the most remote locations.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4253,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-key-atlantic-currents-collapse-century.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-key-atlantic-currents-collapse-century.html','Key Atlantic currents could collapse this century, but the risk is missing from almost all national climate plans','keyatlanticcurrentscouldcollapsethiscenturybuttheriskismissingfromalmostallnationalclimateplans','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T18:20:45Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Icelandic government officials recently met to discuss a dramatic scenario: what if the current that circulates Atlantic waters weakened significantly, or collapsed altogether?',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4254,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-analysis-crdoba-urban-trees-species.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-analysis-crdoba-urban-trees-species.html','Analysis of Córdoba''s urban trees identifies which species best tolerate heat','analysisofcordobasurbantreesidentifieswhichspeciesbesttolerateheat','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T18:00:31Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','The orange tree emerges as one of the most heat-vulnerable trees, according to a University of Córdoba (UCO) study that analyzed 17 tree species found in Córdoba''s urban environment.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4255,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-relief-indonesian-city-weary-haze.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-relief-indonesian-city-weary-haze.html','Rains bring relief to Indonesian city weary of fire haze','rainsbringrelieftoindonesiancitywearyoffirehaze','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T17:41:40Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Residents and firefighters in an Indonesian city long shrouded in a toxic haze brought on by months of wildfires rushed outside dancing and chanting as rain this week brought a welcome respite.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4256,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africa-availability-untapped.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africa-availability-untapped.html','Africa''s water availability sometimes goes untapped','africaswateravailabilitysometimesgoesuntapped','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T17:10:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Water demand in Africa grew by around 26% between 2010 and 2020, yet around 717 million people lacked a piped water supply in 2020, according to research published in Nature. The findings indicate that water scarcity in some parts of Africa may be due to a lack of infrastructure, rather than a lack of available water. The work provides a comprehensive map for water demand in different sectors, highlighting issues that need to be addressed to increase the availability of water in Africa, which forms part of Sustainable Development Goal 6.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4257,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-rainfall-mountain-slopes-rivers-faster.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-rainfall-mountain-slopes-rivers-faster.html','Rainfall can travel from mountain slopes to rivers far faster than expected in mountain catchments','rainfallcantravelfrommountainslopestoriversfarfasterthanexpectedinmountaincatchments','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T16:00:06Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Researchers at University of Tsukuba have found that river responses to rainfall can reach rivers and appear as runoff much more rapidly than previously assumed in mountainous regions. Through continuous monitoring of precipitation and river water levels, they documented rapid rainfall-related responses not only in moderate-relief catchments but also in high-relief catchments with mountain slopes exceeding 500 m in length. The findings provide new insights into runoff generation and flood development in mountainous regions. The paper is published in the Journal of Hydrology.',4.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4258,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-universities-global-biodiversity-goals.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-universities-global-biodiversity-goals.html','How universities can help deliver global biodiversity goals','howuniversitiescanhelpdeliverglobalbiodiversitygoals','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T15:40:06Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','"Taking Action for Nature" was the theme of CoP 17, the UN Biodiversity Conference 2026, which is drawing to a close in Yerevan, Armenia. A new analysis of African national biodiversity strategies and action plans shows how academic and research institutions can respond to this theme and help translate scientific evidence into taking action for nature.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4259,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-underpreparing-severe-climate-uk-overpreparing.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-underpreparing-severe-climate-uk-overpreparing.html','Underpreparing for severe climate risks can cost the UK far more than overpreparing, experts warn','underpreparingforsevereclimateriskscancosttheukfarmorethanoverpreparingexpertswarn','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T15:00:06Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Experts in climate, nature, health, national security, and financial risk have joined forces to call for a more integrated approach to government climate and nature risk assessments. In a commentary published in Nature Climate Change, they warn that conflicting advice can give policymakers very different pictures of the severity of the risks ahead, making it harder to judge how seriously to act and how much to spend.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4260,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-forest.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-forest.html','What makes forest protection work?','whatmakesforestprotectionwork','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T14:40:06Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','A new study from the Institute for Food and Resource Economics at the University of Bonn shows that the success of nature conservation measures depends partly on the credible enforcement of environmental laws.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4261,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-indoor-air-quality-cooling.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-indoor-air-quality-cooling.html','Indoor plants could improve air quality and cooling','indoorplantscouldimproveairqualityandcooling','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T13:40:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','In a study published in Eco-Environment and Health, a team of 35 international experts led by Surrey''s Global Centre for Clean Air Research (GCARE) reviewed several studies from across the world on the performance of indoor greenery.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4262,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-saharan-bacteria-atlantic-amazon-rainforest.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-saharan-bacteria-atlantic-amazon-rainforest.html','Saharan dust carries desert bacteria across the Atlantic to Amazon rainforest','saharandustcarriesdesertbacteriaacrosstheatlantictoamazonrainforest','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T13:20:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Viewed under a microscope, the air above the Amazon rainforest teems with activity. It is full of pollen, fungal spores, tiny plant particles and bacteria. Normally, the composition of this bioaerosol community is quite stable. A team that included researchers from the University of Graz, the Max Planck Institute for Chemistry in Mainz and Brazilian partner institutions has now shown that microorganisms are transported across the Atlantic along with desert dust from the Sahara.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4263,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-children-brunt-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-children-brunt-climate.html','Children bear the brunt of climate change, warns new report','childrenbearthebruntofclimatechangewarnsnewreport','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T12:40:01Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Climate change is posing an increasing threat to the health and well-being of children and young people in Australia and New Zealand, with rising exposure to heat waves and widespread climate-related distress among young Australians, according to the annual MJA–Lancet Countdown Oceania report.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4264,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ancient-oceans-carbon-export-fish.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ancient-oceans-carbon-export-fish.html','As ancient oceans warmed, carbon export and fish productivity surged','asancientoceanswarmedcarbonexportandfishproductivitysurged','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T11:20:02Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Ocean fish stocks have been increasingly exploited over time, and remote sensing shows they have declined in the open ocean over the last three decades, particularly in the tropics. But it''s uncertain how fish populations will fare in the future, as climate models show both increases and decreases in global trends by the end of this century.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4265,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-philippine-island-seas-nature-concrete.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-philippine-island-seas-nature-concrete.html','Philippine island fights rising seas with nature, not concrete','philippineislandfightsrisingseaswithnaturenotconcrete','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T09:23:55Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','For years, residents of a flood-prone Philippine island north of Manila have elevated their homes atop layer upon layer of concrete, only to see floodwaters threaten them again.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4266,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-declare-climate-health-emergency.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-declare-climate-health-emergency.html','Why the WHO must declare climate change a public health emergency','whythewhomustdeclareclimatechangeapublichealthemergency','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-29T08:55:47Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Across the world, people have felt the impacts of climate change firsthand. The Northern Hemisphere has had another record-breaking summer season of heat and extreme weather events. The Southern Hemisphere now faces a summer of heat waves, flooding, wildfires and droughts. This is driven by decades of climate change, now supercharged by an unprecedented El Niño—the warm phase of the El Niño Southern Oscillation, which is a cyclical warming and cooling of the Pacific Ocean driven by changing trade winds, with global impacts on the climate.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(4267,'https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1022519/google-gemini-file-editing-documents-pdfs','https://theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1022519/google-gemini-file-editing-documents-pdfs','You can generate and edit Google Docs files directly in Gemini chats now','youcangenerateandeditgoogledocsfilesdirectlyingeminichatsnow','theverge_ai','The Verge - AI','en','2026-10-29T18:45:31Z','2026-10-30T04:00:49Z','Now you can generate, preview, and edit PDFs, Google Docs, Google Sheets, slide decks, Markdown files, TXT files, and code directly within Google Gemini. The new file editing capabilities are available starting today for all Gemini users, and while PDF previews/editing works on both the web and mobile, all other file formats are only in […]',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
